  "stayLabel": "Stay on St Ebba’s",
  "briefing": [
    "Seahouses, Northumberland. Wednesday 1 October 2025. Three miles out to sea lies St Ebba’s Isle: a seabird reserve with a ruined chapel, a lighthouse, and two rangers who live out there from April to October. Day visitors cross on Dunn’s Boats’ Ebba Belle. Sunday was the last landing day of the season.",
    "A rare bird, a Siberian rubythroat, brought forty people across on Sunday. That afternoon, at the island’s Landing, forty return tickets were scanned onto the last boat. At Seahouses, thirty-nine people got off. The missing passenger was Nell Ashby, 39, Head of Reserves at the Northumberland Seabird Trust. On Monday morning the lifeboat found her body in the sea below the island’s north end.",
    "The police believe she went up to the open upper deck as the boat rounded the north end in a heavy swell, and fell overboard unseen. At 16:22 she had messaged her husband, Tom, to get on and save her a seat, and her ticket was scanned four minutes later. The Maritime and Coastguard Agency is investigating Dunn’s Boats. Rob Dunn, the skipper, has had his licence suspended, and online he’s ‘the man who lost a passenger and didn’t notice’.",
    "Nell’s sister, Bea Lowther, has hired you. ‘Nell was sick on every boat she ever got on. She sat inside, by the door, every single time. She texted Tom to save her a seat inside. Inside! And they want me to believe she climbed a ladder to the top deck in a swell?’",
    "The trust has given you a crossing on its work boat. The two rangers are closing the island for the winter and leave on Friday. Everyone who was on St Ebba’s on Sunday afternoon is still close by."
  ],
